(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-exam-1-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Pharmacology I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How do macrolides interact with other drugs, and in what order of severity?","choices":["They chelate divalent cations, reducing absorption of co-administered drugs","They displace substrates from plasma protein binding sites","Metabolites bind CYP3A subclass enzymes forming an inactive complex, raising levels of substrates such as carbamazepine, cyclosporine, digoxin, midazolam and theophylline, with erythromycin greater than clarithromycin greater than azithromycin","They induce CYP3A subclass enzymes, lowering levels of substrates such as carbamazepine, cyclosporine, digoxin, midazolam and theophylline, with azithromycin greater than clarithromycin greater than erythromycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The ranking is the practical point: azithromycin is the one to reach for when interactions matter. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"From what source is amphotericin B derived?","choices":["Penicillium griseofulvum","Bacillus polymyxa","The soil bacterium Amycolatopsis orientalis","A soil Streptomycetaceae organism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which places it alongside several antibacterials in being a natural product of a soil microbe. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"What is the defining clinical advantage of aztreonam?","choices":["No cross-reactivity with beta-lactams, so it can be used in truly penicillin-allergic patients","The broadest spectrum of any beta-lactam, covering Gram-positives, Gram-negatives and anaerobes","Oral bioavailability allowing outpatient completion of therapy","Reliable central nervous system penetration for meningitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That property is what makes a monobactam worth having despite its narrow Gram-negative-only spectrum. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Why does the lecture counsel caution with dobutamine in atrial fibrillation?","choices":["It causes bronchoconstriction","It depletes cardiac catecholamine stores","It increases atrioventricular conduction","It slows sinoatrial firing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which can accelerate the ventricular response. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Which retinoid does the lecture pair with Kaposi sarcoma?","choices":["Adapalene","Alitretinoin","Bexarotene","Tazarotene"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bexarotene is the one paired with T-cell lymphoma. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"When should a vancomycin trough be drawn?","choices":["Immediately before the second dose","At any point within the dosing interval, provided the exact time of the draw is recorded","15 to 30 minutes before the next scheduled dose, usually around the third or fourth dose","30 minutes after the end of the infusion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Drawing around the third or fourth dose is what makes the level reflect steady state. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What distinguishes a bacteriostatic drug from a bactericidal drug, as the lecture defines them?","choices":["Bacteriostatic requires a higher concentration to act; bactericidal acts at any concentration above zero","Bacteriostatic agents are given orally and bactericidal agents are given intravenously","Bacteriostatic inhibits a vital growth pathway without directly causing death; bactericidal disrupts function enough that death occurs","Bacteriostatic works only on Gram-positive organisms; bactericidal works on both Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture's split is about whether the drug itself kills, not about how fast it works. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about starting acyclovir within the first 24 hours of chickenpox?","choices":["It cures the infection and prevents later reactivation as shingles","It has no effect unless combined with valacyclovir","It prevents transmission to contacts but does not alter the illness","It shortens the acute illness but does not cure the infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the virus is not eradicated, which is why reactivation as shingles remains possible later. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"What does the lecture say norepinephrine acts as?","choices":["Both a neurotransmitter and a hormone","A neurotransmitter only, with no hormonal role","A hormone only, released from the adrenal cortex","An inhibitory transmitter in pain pathways of the spinal cord"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it also regulates normal brain processes, usually excitatory. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What does the lecture say happens when epinephrine is given to a patient on a beta blocker?","choices":["There is no clinically important interaction","Alpha effects are left unopposed","Both alpha and beta effects are abolished","The blood pressure falls sharply"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blocking the vasodilating beta side leaves only vasoconstriction. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Which electrolyte and renal effects accompany amphotericin B?","choices":["Isolated hepatotoxicity with no renal involvement","Bone marrow suppression as the dose-limiting toxicity","Hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia, hypotension, uraemia in about 80 percent with decreased filtration, and renal tubule damage mitigated by hydration with normal saline","Hyperkalaemia and hypercalcaemia with a rising glomerular filtration rate, hypertension, and tubular hypertrophy that resolves without any need for hydration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The saline hydration is the practical countermeasure worth remembering. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"What route and dosing consideration apply to oseltamivir?","choices":["Available intravenously only, with hepatic adjustment","Available orally and intravenously, with no adjustment needed","Available as an inhaled powder only","Available orally only, and requiring renal dose adjustment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Renal adjustment is a consideration rather than a dose to memorise. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 127"},{"q":"Which emergency indications does the lecture give for epinephrine?","choices":["Hypertensive emergency","Chronic heart failure","Anaphylactic shock and cardiac arrest","Cardiogenic and septic shock"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside acute reversal of bronchospasm. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"How does the lecture characterise dopamine?","choices":["Multiple functions by location, and usually inhibitory","Purely excitatory, controlling muscle movement in spinal cord neurons","The major inhibitory transmitter of the brain","Involved chiefly in mood, appetite and sensory perception"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which contrasts with acetylcholine being usually excitatory. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which of these is a LOCAL adverse effect of topical steroids in the lecture, rather than a systemic one?","choices":["Adrenal suppression","Cataracts","Growth retardation in children","Skin atrophy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Local effects are skin atrophy, acne, rosacea and allergic dermatitis related to the vehicle. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How does flucytosine achieve selectivity for fungal cells?","choices":["It is converted to 5-fluorouridine by cytosine deaminase, an enzyme human cells lack, and then inhibits thymidylate synthase","It binds ergosterol, which human membranes do not contain","It inhibits a fungal cytochrome P450 enzyme with no human counterpart","It is actively pumped into fungal cells by a membrane transporter that is entirely absent from human cells and tissues"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The missing human enzyme is the whole basis of selectivity. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Why is ketoconazole rarely used now?","choices":["It does not enter the central nervous system and it inhibits cytochrome P450 3A4, producing significant drug interactions","It is available only intravenously and causes severe infusion reactions requiring routine premedication before every dose","It is the only azole ineffective against Candida","It causes visual disturbance in about 30 percent of patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and itraconazole with its wider spectrum largely replaced it. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Most topical acne agents cause skin irritation. What does the lecture say that leads to, and how does it advise starting?","choices":["It confirms the drug is working, so maintain the starting strength","It requires an immediate switch to systemic therapy, because irritation means the topical agent has failed","It is prevented entirely by using an alcoholic solution","It can lead to patient discontinuation, so start with lower strength and increase gradually"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Anticipating the irritation is what keeps a patient on therapy. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What loading dose does the lecture give for vancomycin in critically ill patients?","choices":["15 to 20 milligrams per kilogram, rounded to the nearest 500 milligrams","7 milligrams per kilogram once daily","1 gram regardless of weight","25 to 30 milligrams per kilogram, rounded to the nearest 500 milligrams"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The loading dose exists to reach the target trough quickly rather than waiting through several maintenance doses. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which antiviral approach does the acyclovir family represent?","choices":["Inhibiting viral assembly","Inhibiting nucleic acid synthesis","Inhibiting viral release","Blocking uncoating after entry"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the drug is incorporated into viral DNA and terminates the chain. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"What is mupirocin's mechanism in the lecture?","choices":["It interrupts the bacterial cytoplasmic membrane","It inhibits fungal ergosterol production and so prevents cell wall synthesis","It binds bacterial transfer RNA, preventing protein synthesis","It prevents bacterial cell wall synthesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the lecture notes it is not absorbed though it may irritate mucous membranes. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which azole penetrates the central nervous system and is therefore used in cryptococcal meningitis?","choices":["Caspofungin","Fluconazole","Ketoconazole","Itraconazole"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It also has the best oral absorption of the group and is excreted in the urine, which suits it to candidiasis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"What is the dual mechanism of action of the fluoroquinolones?","choices":["Inhibition of bacterial DNA gyrase, also called topoisomerase II, forming a quinolone-DNA-gyrase complex with induced DNA cleavage, plus inhibition of topoisomerase IV","Inhibition of the 30S and 50S ribosomal subunits simultaneously","Sulfamethoxazole inhibits dihydrofolate reductase and blocks conversion of dihydrofolic acid to tetrahydrofolic acid; trimethoprim inhibits tetrahydropteroic acid synthetase upstream of it","Inhibition of cell wall cross-linking and of beta-lactamase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture notes the topoisomerase IV mechanism is poorly understood. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What are the two limits the lecture places on pralidoxime?","choices":["It must be given orally, and it is inactivated by food","It reverses muscarinic but not nicotinic signs","It does not enter the brain, and cannot overcome reversible inhibitors","It works only in children, and only within one hour of exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It reactivates inhibited acetylcholinesterase but only within those limits. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe the nicotinic receptor?","choices":["An intracellular receptor that binds acetylcholine after it crosses the membrane","Five subunits forming a ligand-gated ion channel opened by two acetylcholine molecules","A seven-transmembrane receptor coupled to a G protein and a second messenger","An enzyme-linked receptor that phosphorylates intracellular targets on binding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is found in the central nervous system, adrenal medulla, autonomic ganglia and neuromuscular junction. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which antisecretory use does the lecture give for atropine?","choices":["Reducing bronchial secretions in asthma maintenance","Preventing sweating in axillary hyperhidrosis","Blocking respiratory tract secretions before surgery","Reducing gastric acid secretion in peptic ulcer disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and glycopyrrolate is used similarly before surgery. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"How do sulfamethoxazole and trimethoprim block folate synthesis at two different steps?","choices":["Sulfamethoxazole inhibits conversion of para-aminobenzoic acid to dihydrofolic acid via tetrahydropteroic acid synthetase; trimethoprim inhibits conversion of dihydrofolic acid to tetrahydrofolic acid via dihydrofolate reductase","Sulfamethoxazole inhibits the conversion of dihydrofolic acid to tetrahydrofolic acid via dihydrofolate reductase; trimethoprim inhibits the conversion of para-aminobenzoic acid to dihydrofolic acid via tetrahydropteroic acid synthetase","Both inhibit dihydrofolate reductase, at different binding sites","Sulfamethoxazole blocks folate absorption from the gut; trimethoprim blocks its renal reabsorption"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Two sequential steps in one pathway, which is what makes the combination synergistic. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Which uses does the lecture list for propranolol beyond hypertension?","choices":["Pheochromocytoma and clonidine withdrawal","Migraine, hyperthyroidism, angina and infarction","Glaucoma, asthma and benign prostatic hyperplasia","Shock, cardiac arrest and anaphylaxis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and after infarction it reduces infarct size and sudden death. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"Which two problems does the lecture attach to intralesional steroid injection of an inflammatory nodule?","choices":["Photosensitivity and severe sunburn","Systemic absorption leading to adrenal suppression, and local tissue atrophy","Permanent hyperpigmentation and keloid formation","Bleaching of hair and clothing, together with staining of the surrounding skin at the injection site"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both are consequences of injecting a steroid into the lesion. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Itraconazole is useful in which infections?","choices":["Zygomycete infection including Mucor and Rhizopus","Systemic aspergillosis as first-line therapy","Blastomycosis, histoplasmosis, onychomycosis, and febrile neutropenic patients not responding to antibiotics","Cryptococcal meningitis and prophylaxis against relapse in advanced acquired immunodeficiency syndrome"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It has an active metabolite and a wider spectrum than the ketoconazole it replaced. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which adverse effect does the lecture attach to atracurium specifically?","choices":["Malignant hyperthermia in susceptible people","Prolonged paralysis from pseudocholinesterase deficiency","Rebound tachycardia on withdrawal","Histamine release, with a fall in blood pressure and flushing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it was replaced by its isomer cisatracurium for having fewer adverse effects. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What term does the lecture give as synonymous with an indirect-acting cholinergic agonist?","choices":["Choline ester","Anticholinesterase","Parasympatholytic","Antimuscarinic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They raise acetylcholine by blocking its degradation rather than binding the receptor. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which allylamine is available both orally and topically, and which is topical only?","choices":["Naftifine is oral and topical; terbinafine is topical only","Both are available only topically","Both are available only orally","Terbinafine is oral and topical; naftifine is topical only"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both inhibit squalene epoxidase and are used for superficial dermatophyte infection. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"Which poisoning does the lecture say physostigmine treats?","choices":["Overdose of anticholinergic drugs such as atropine","Overdose of a cholinesterase inhibitor insecticide","Beta blocker overdose","Opioid overdose"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It raises acetylcholine to compete with the blocking drug. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Why is influenza treatment framed around a season in this lecture?","choices":["Flu season runs roughly October to March, which is when the illness is circulating","The drug is only manufactured and distributed during those particular months","Resistance disappears outside the season","Renal dosing requirements change with the season"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it frames when an influenza-like illness should raise suspicion in the first place. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"Several antivirals in this lecture are prodrugs. Which set is correct?","choices":["Acyclovir, penciclovir, ganciclovir and zanamivir","Amphotericin B, caspofungin and griseofulvin","Metronidazole, linezolid and daptomycin","Valacyclovir, famciclovir, valganciclovir and oseltamivir"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Improving oral absorption is a recurring design theme across the whole antiviral section. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Why must renal function be watched during acyclovir therapy?","choices":["The drug is nephrotoxic through direct tubular necrosis","Its vehicle accumulates in renal impairment","It causes interstitial nephritis as a hypersensitivity reaction","The drug crystallises in the renal tubule, so hydration must be maintained"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a physical rather than a toxic mechanism of renal injury. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Which uses does the lecture list for phenylephrine?","choices":["Nasal decongestant, eye redness and septic shock","Asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Hypertension and opiate withdrawal","Glaucoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Which vascular beds does dopamine dilate, and through which receptors?","choices":["Renal and splanchnic, through dopaminergic receptors","Coronary and cerebral, through beta-two receptors","Skeletal muscle beds, through alpha-two receptors","Pulmonary vessels, through dopaminergic receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is distinct from its beta-one cardiac and high-dose alpha-one effects. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which adverse effect of benzoyl peroxide most needs to be raised with a patient before they start it?","choices":["It can bleach hair, clothing and bedding","It causes a permanent blue-grey discolouration of the skin","It commonly causes systemic hypersensitivity reactions","It stains the skin orange for the duration of therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. This is the practical warning that prevents a patient ruining clothes and abandoning the drug. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A patient develops pustular inflammation on the trunk a few weeks after starting a systemic corticosteroid. What does the lecture say happens when the steroid is stopped?","choices":["Immediate and complete resolution of the eruption","Conversion of the pustules into open comedones","No change, because the eruption is independent of the drug","An initial worsening, because inflammation increases"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and warning the patient of this in advance is what stops them abandoning the plan. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which azole has the best oral absorption, and how is it eliminated?","choices":["Posaconazole, excreted by the lungs","Fluconazole, excreted in the urine","Ketoconazole, excreted in the bile","Itraconazole, excreted unchanged in the faeces"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and its urinary excretion is part of why a single dose treats vaginal candidiasis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"What does the lecture say distinguishes dobutamine from other sympathomimetics?","choices":["It produces no tolerance with prolonged use","It barely raises myocardial oxygen demand","It has no effect on atrioventricular conduction","It can be given orally"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which matters in a failing heart. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Why is tyramine dangerous in a patient taking a monoamine oxidase inhibitor?","choices":["It blocks reuptake of norepinephrine at the terminal","It is converted into amphetamine in the liver","It directly stimulates alpha-one receptors","Monoamine oxidase normally destroys it in the gut"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is found in fermented foods such as cheese and wine. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"What is the polymyxin mechanism of action?","choices":["Binding the 30S ribosomal subunit and preventing transfer RNA from occupying the A site of the bacterial ribosome during chain elongation and protein assembly","Covalent inactivation of penicillin-binding proteins","A detergent-like interaction with the lipopolysaccharide of the Gram-negative outer membrane, displacing magnesium and calcium and disrupting the membrane","Inhibition of 1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase in the cell wall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is why the spectrum is broad Gram-negative coverage — the target only exists there. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"Cefazolin and cephalexin are first generation cephalosporins. What is their activity profile?","choices":["Excellent Gram-positive activity with no Enterococcus coverage, and some Gram-negative coverage of Escherichia coli, Proteus and Klebsiella","Excellent Gram-negative activity including Pseudomonas aeruginosa, with limited Gram-positive coverage and reliable activity against Enterococcus","Broad coverage including anaerobes and methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus","Activity restricted to atypical organisms such as Legionella and Mycoplasma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The absence of Enterococcus coverage is a recurring theme across the whole cephalosporin class. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What should a patient be told about taking griseofulvin with food?","choices":["It should be taken with an antacid to reduce gastric irritation","Absorption is increased by a high-fat meal, because the drug is very lipid soluble","It must be taken on an empty stomach, since food chelates and inactivates the drug","Food has no effect on absorption"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is effective against numerous dermatophytes but not Candida, acting fungistatically. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Why does a local anaesthetic solution often contain epinephrine?","choices":["It prevents an allergic reaction to the anaesthetic","It causes vasoconstriction at the injection site","It speeds systemic absorption of the anaesthetic","It provides additional analgesia of its own"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and applied topically it also helps control capillary oozing. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"How does the lecture divide the efferent division of the peripheral nervous system?","choices":["Into the afferent system, carrying signals to the centre, and the enteric system, acting independently","Into the sympathetic and parasympathetic systems only, with no somatic component","Into the brain and the spinal cord, which together form the outflow","Somatic, under voluntary control, and autonomic, under involuntary control"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Afferent carries signals to the central nervous system; efferent carries them away and splits this way. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What does imiquimod do, per the lecture?","choices":["It corrects abnormal follicular keratinization, reduces bacterial counts within the follicle, and dampens the inflammatory response","It stimulates peripheral mononuclear cells to release interferon alpha and macrophages to produce tumour necrosis factor alpha and interleukins","It inhibits T cell, mast cell and keratinocyte activation","It is converted to benzoic acid after crossing the stratum corneum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is an immunomodulator rather than a directly cytotoxic agent. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What does the lecture warn happens if oxymetazoline is used beyond three days?","choices":["Permanent loss of the sense of smell","Systemic hypertension requiring treatment","Nasal septal perforation","Rhinitis medicamentosa, or rebound congestion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is the practical reason for a hard stop on a decongestant spray. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"Which problem does the lecture specifically attach to topical neomycin?","choices":["It causes hypopigmentation","It is inactivated by benzoyl peroxide","It frequently causes sensitisation","It bleaches hair and clothing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the class can also accumulate systemically. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Why does the lecture include a sedating antihistamine in the non-pharmacologic advice for a child with atopic dermatitis?","choices":["To treat the secondary skin infections","To lower serum immunoglobulin E","To reduce night-time itching","To reverse the skin barrier dysfunction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The sedating property is being used for the itch that disrupts sleep. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"At what dose does dopamine begin to cause vasoconstriction, and through which receptor?","choices":["At very high doses, through alpha-one","At low doses, through alpha-one","At low doses, through beta-two","At any dose, through dopaminergic receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, whereas its cardiac effect is beta-one and its renal effect dopaminergic. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"From what is acetylcholine synthesised, per the lecture's diagram?","choices":["Tyrosine and dopamine","Glutamate and glycine","Tryptophan and serotonin","Acetyl coenzyme A and choline"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and choline is recovered by recycling after the transmitter is degraded. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which two antiviral agents in this lecture are given intravenously as the parent compound?","choices":["Valganciclovir and valacyclovir, given intravenously in transplant patients","Penciclovir and ganciclovir, each with an oral prodrug available","Valacyclovir and famciclovir, each with an intravenous prodrug","Oseltamivir and acyclovir, neither having an oral form"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — famciclovir and valganciclovir respectively are the better-absorbed oral forms. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Carbapenems are the drug of choice for which infections?","choices":["Uncomplicated urinary tract infection and outpatient sinusitis","Atypical pneumonia caused by Mycoplasma or Legionella","Multidrug-resistant Gram-negative infections, extended-spectrum beta-lactamase producing bacteria, nosocomial infections, and meningitis","Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia, infective endocarditis, vertebral osteomyelitis, and prosthetic joint infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Their resistance to hydrolysis by beta-lactamases is what makes them the answer to extended-spectrum producers. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Azoles and allylamines both reduce ergosterol. At which enzyme does each act?","choices":["Both inhibit lanosterol demethylase, differing only in binding site","Azoles inhibit lanosterol demethylase; allylamines inhibit squalene epoxidase","Azoles inhibit squalene epoxidase; allylamines inhibit lanosterol demethylase","Azoles inhibit 1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase; allylamines inhibit lanosterol demethylase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Same pathway, different steps — which is why both end in decreased ergosterol. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Which terms does the lecture give as synonymous with cholinergic antagonists?","choices":["Anticholinergics, parasympatholytics or antimuscarinics","Cholinomimetics and parasympathomimetics","Sympatholytics and adrenergic blockers","Anticholinesterases and indirect-acting agents"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, while the agonists are cholinomimetics or parasympathomimetics. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Ampicillin and amoxicillin are the drugs of choice for which set of indications?","choices":["Pseudomonas aeruginosa pneumonia, nosocomial intra-abdominal infection, and polymicrobial infection in the critically ill patient","Syphilis, gas gangrene, and meningococcal disease","Enterococcus, Listeria, endocarditis prophylaxis, upper respiratory tract infection, and community-acquired pneumonia at high dose","Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia and osteomyelitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aminopenicillins add some Gram-negative aerobic activity to the natural penicillin spectrum but still lack Staphylococcus coverage. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 19"}]};
